THE BEAM/PHOTO BUG, which I got wrong twice and should not be repeated:

  The photo column had z-40 and the rays z-30. That looks right in isolation
  but the photo column is nested inside the hero grid, and that grid is z-10.
  A child CANNOT escape its parent's stacking context — the whole grid,
  z-40 child and all, painted beneath the z-30 rays.

  I then "verified" it by taking an element screenshot of the figure and read
  the beam crossing it as the photo's own dappled sunlight. Two mistakes
  reinforcing each other, across two passes, while the user told me three
  times it was still wrong.

  Fixed STRUCTURALLY, not with more z-index: SideRays now lives inside the
  text column, so it cannot reach the photo whatever the stacking does. There
  is an assertion in the check script that the two bounding boxes never
  intersect, run at five widths.

  Lesson: when an overlay is crossing something it shouldn't, check the
  ancestor chain for a stacking context before touching z-index, and verify
  with a geometric assertion rather than by eye.

SVELTE UPDATE LOOP: binding twelve children two-way into one object
(bind:flipped={flipped[index]}) while a reactive statement reassigns that same
object hangs SSR and wedges the dev server — every route times out, not just
the offending one. If the whole server stops responding after a component
edit, suspect a reactive loop rather than a crashed process. Children own
their own state now; {#key active} resets them.

